<div class="mb-10"><h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Benchmarks: Soli vs Ruby</h1>
<p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-6">Reproducible per-operation numbers comparing Soli's production engine against Ruby. The scripts ship in <code class="text-cyan-400">bench/cross-language/</code> so you can re-run them yourself.</p>
<div class="rounded-xl border border-amber-500/30 bg-amber-500/5 p-5 mb-8"><p class="text-gray-300 text-sm leading-relaxed"><strong class="text-amber-400">Read this first.</strong> Benchmarks are easy to rig and easy to get wrong &mdash; this page has already been corrected once (see <a href="#corrections" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">Corrections</a>). Everything below is best-of-7 on <strong class="text-white">byte-identical inputs</strong> generated by the same LCG in both languages, and Ruby is credited with its <strong class="text-white">best</strong> result across three modes, not its slowest. Where Soli loses, the number is printed exactly as measured.</p></div></div>
<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Setup</h2>
<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 mb-6"><table class="w-full text-left text-sm"><tbody class="text-gray-400 divide-y divide-white/10">
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4 text-white font-medium w-32">Soli</td><td class="py-3 px-4">production bytecode VM (<code class="text-cyan-400">soli --vm</code>)</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4 text-white font-medium w-32">Ruby</td><td class="py-3 px-4">4.0.6 &mdash; default, <code class="text-cyan-400">--yjit</code>, <code class="text-cyan-400">--zjit</code></td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4 text-white font-medium w-32">Method</td><td class="py-3 px-4">best-of-7 per operation, warmed once first</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4 text-white font-medium w-32">Data</td><td class="py-3 px-4">n = 20,000; identical LCG-generated inputs in both languages</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4 text-white font-medium w-32">Machine</td><td class="py-3 px-4">idle 16-core x86-64 Linux</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p class="text-gray-400 mb-8"><strong class="text-white">ratio</strong> is Soli &divide; Ruby's <em>best</em> mode. <strong class="text-amber-400">Below 1.00 means Soli is faster.</strong></p>
<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Summary</h2>
<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 mb-6"><table class="w-full text-left text-sm"><thead class="bg-white/5 text-gray-300"><tr><th class="py-3 px-4">Category</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">Geometric mean</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">Soli wins</th></tr></thead><tbody class="text-gray-400 divide-y divide-white/10">
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4 text-white">String</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-emerald-400">0.36&times; &mdash; Soli <strong>2.8&times; faster</strong></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">11/13</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4 text-white">Aggregate</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-emerald-400">0.39&times; &mdash; Soli <strong>2.5&times; faster</strong></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">11/11</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4 text-white">Array</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-amber-300">1.24&times; &mdash; Ruby 1.2&times; faster</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">6/16</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4 text-white">Hash</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-amber-300">2.19&times; &mdash; Ruby 2.2&times; faster</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">2/11</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4 text-white">DateTime</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-amber-300">2.58&times; &mdash; Ruby 2.6&times; faster</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1/9</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4 text-white">Numeric</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-amber-300">4.13&times; &mdash; Ruby 4.1&times; faster</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0/3</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4 text-white">Control</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-amber-300">4.79&times; &mdash; Ruby 4.8&times; faster</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0/2</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4 text-white">Duration</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-amber-300">9.16&times; &mdash; Ruby 9.2&times; faster</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0/2</td></tr>
<tr class="bg-white/5"><td class="py-3 px-4 text-white font-bold">Overall</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-white font-bold">1.14&times;</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-white font-bold">31/67</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">The split is not random &mdash; it tracks <strong class="text-white">where the work happens</strong>:</p>
<ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-400 space-y-2 mb-6"><li><strong class="text-emerald-400">Inside Rust</strong> &mdash; string transforms, <code class="text-cyan-400">flatten</code>, <code class="text-cyan-400">sort</code>, <code class="text-cyan-400">DateTime.parse</code>, and the whole Aggregate family &mdash; Soli wins, often by 3&ndash;30&times;.</li><li><strong class="text-amber-300">Per-element in interpreted code</strong> &mdash; arithmetic loops, function calls, blocks, hash iteration &mdash; Ruby wins by 2&ndash;4&times;, because its JIT compiles those loops to machine code.</li></ul>
<div class="rounded-xl border border-emerald-500/30 bg-emerald-500/5 p-5 mb-10"><p class="text-gray-300 text-sm leading-relaxed"><strong class="text-emerald-400">The practical lesson is the Aggregate row.</strong> <code class="text-cyan-400">rows.sum_by("amount")</code> and <code class="text-cyan-400">rows.reduce(fn(a, r) { return a + r["amount"] }, 0)</code> compute the same thing, but the first stays in Rust while the second re-enters the interpreter 20,000 times &mdash; a <strong class="text-white">235&times;</strong> difference, and the difference between beating Ruby and losing to it. Prefer the built-in.</p></div>
<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Aggregate</h2><p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">Field-keyed aggregates: <code class="text-cyan-400">rows.sum_by("amount")</code> rather than <code class="text-cyan-400">rows.reduce(fn(a, r) ...)</code>. Because the field name is data rather than a callback, the whole traversal stays in Rust and never re-enters the interpreter. Ruby has no non-block form of these, so its versions pay a block call per element &mdash; which is precisely the cost being avoided.</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 mb-10"><table class="w-full text-left text-sm"><thead class="bg-white/5 text-gray-300"><tr><th class="py-3 px-4">Operation</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">Soli</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">Ruby</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">+YJIT</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">+ZJIT</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">Ratio</th></tr></thead><tbody class="text-gray-400 divide-y divide-white/10">
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">min_by</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.339</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1.362</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1.124</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1.380</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-emerald-400 font-semibold">0.30&times;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">filter_by</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.499</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1.672</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1.764</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1.598</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-emerald-400 font-semibold">0.31&times;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">avg_by</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.321</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1.183</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1.013</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1.122</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-emerald-400 font-semibold">0.32&times;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">sum_by</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.323</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1.260</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.988</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1.115</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-emerald-400 font-semibold">0.33&times;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">find_by</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.355</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1.312</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1.064</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1.128</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-emerald-400 font-semibold">0.33&times;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">index_by</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.798</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">2.528</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">2.195</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">2.425</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-emerald-400 font-semibold">0.36&times;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">count_by</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.826</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">2.631</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">2.238</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">2.407</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-emerald-400 font-semibold">0.37&times;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">max_by</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.524</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1.310</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1.264</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1.408</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-emerald-400 font-semibold">0.41&times;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">group_by</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1.016</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">2.136</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1.889</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1.976</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-emerald-400 font-semibold">0.54&times;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">tally</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.192</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.393</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.334</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.396</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-emerald-400 font-semibold">0.58&times;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">uniq_by</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.859</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1.602</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1.419</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1.715</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-emerald-400 font-semibold">0.61&times;</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-3">String</h2><p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">Soli's strings are Rust <code class="text-cyan-400">EcoString</code>s and the methods are native code with no per-element interpretation.</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 mb-10"><table class="w-full text-left text-sm"><thead class="bg-white/5 text-gray-300"><tr><th class="py-3 px-4">Operation</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">Soli</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">Ruby</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">+YJIT</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">+ZJIT</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">Ratio</th></tr></thead><tbody class="text-gray-400 divide-y divide-white/10">
